FreeSDN Valkey HA: topology, persistence and upgrade limits
========================================================

This is a single-Docker-host process-failover topology. It does not tolerate
loss of that host. Three Sentinels on one host are not independent failure
domains. PostgreSQL promotion is still manual. Do not advertise this preset
as multi-host enterprise HA or promise zero data loss.

Addressing
----------
Use .env.max.example with docker-compose.yml + docker-compose.ha.yml. The five
Valkey/Sentinel addresses are static and must be inside NETWORK_SUBNET, outside
NETWORK_IP_RANGE (the dynamic allocation pool), distinct from each other and
from the gateway. Defaults use 172.30.0.0/16, dynamic 172.30.0.0/17, and static
172.30.255.10/.11/.21/.22/.23. Choose a non-overlapping network for your site;
change NETWORK_SUBNET, NETWORK_IP_RANGE, FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS and all HA_*_IP
values together. Docker rejects invalid/out-of-subnet/colliding allocations.
Existing Docker networks must be recreated in a planned maintenance window to
change their allocation pool. Do not change live HA node addresses in place.

Numeric addressing is deliberate: Docker removes a stopped service's DNS
record, and Sentinel's synchronous resolver can then stall its event loop and
trigger TILT. All data nodes and Sentinels announce their reserved addresses;
application discovery and Celery Sentinel endpoints use those same addresses.
No Redis/Sentinel ports are published to the host. The existing application
network is retained, including for the opt-in VPN network namespace.

Durable roles and elections
--------------------------
Each of the five nodes has its own named /data volume. Private files under
/data/ha store its initial configuration and credentials. Sentinel rewrites
its configuration as elections occur. Data nodes accept CONFIG REWRITE from
Sentinel to persist promotion/demotion. Restart never regenerates those files
or reinstates a bootstrap replicaof directive. Both data nodes have matching
authentication, memory policy, persistence and destructive-command restrictions.
Keep all five volumes and their identity when replacing containers.

Changing the configured password without coordinated rotation is refused.
Credential rotation requires a planned outage, stopping application writers
and all five nodes, backing up the five volumes privately, and replacing the
requirepass/masterauth/sentinel auth-pass values and /data/ha/password on all
nodes consistently before restarting. Retain role/epoch state; do not delete
configuration files to force initialization. Files contain secrets. This manual
rotation procedure has not yet been qualified as a supported online operation.

Existing installations: do not run an image-only upgrade
-------------------------------------------------------
The previous overlay generated Sentinel configuration in /tmp on each start
and gave the replica no explicit named data volume. It may already have a
different elected primary from the service named redis. Existing AOF/RDB data
without the new HA state marker is therefore refused, not silently adopted.

Before upgrading, inventory the actual elected primary, replication status,
broker queues in DB 0/1/2, Sentinel state and every data volume (including anonymous ones).
The former Sentinel URL list specified /1 or /2 only on its final endpoint.
Producers and workers could therefore select different databases after a
connection change. The corrected endpoints consistently use broker DB 1 and
result DB 2; do not switch databases without reconciling old queues/results.
Fence writers and workers, record in-flight jobs, verify application/database
backups, then preserve offline copies of both data volumes, all configuration
and exact old images. Queue/RDB recovery needs a separately rehearsed procedure
which identifies the authoritative dataset and accounts for in-flight tasks.
Do not discard the old replica's anonymous volume or assume the redis service
contains the newest data. Do not attach an old volume to a fresh bootstrap
primary, or use down -v/prune during an upgrade.

A read-only offline review tool is available as scripts/inventory_legacy_queues.py.
Read QUEUE-INVENTORY before preparing a disposable copy. It inventories all
DB0/1/2 queues, reserved deliveries and results, but neither loads/verifies the
backup nor authorizes replay. Unknown formats remain explicit blockers.

An automated in-place migration from the old HA preset is NOT qualified. Use
this revised topology for fresh isolated qualification until an operator has
reviewed and rehearsed the site-specific migration. Loss of a node's volume
also needs operator-controlled rejoin; a new empty primary volume must not
be bootstrapped against a surviving elected primary. Preserve the evidence
and backups and restore separately. Do not reset the Sentinel quorum.

Durability and partition limits
------------------------------
Replication is asynchronous; AOF uses appendfsync everysec. Acknowledged writes
can be lost during faults. A partitioned old primary may accept writes until
clients reconnect to the elected one, and those writes can later be discarded.
This two-data-node preset favors continued writes after one node fails; it
does not enforce min-replicas-to-write=1, which would prevent writes on the
survivor until the other node returns. Do not use it as a consensus store for
irreversible operations or claim partition fencing/zero-RPO. Durable operation
records and idempotency must live in the primary database.

Qualification
-------------
scripts/check_valkey_ha.py creates only a disposable internal-network project
with synthetic data and no host ports. It tests the shipped Compose definitions,
actual stop/SIGKILL, existing sync/async application clients, persisted elections,
container replacement, reverse failover, rejoin and refusal without quorum.
It never takes over an existing installation. Its timings are laboratory
observations, not an SLA, load-tested RTO or a customer RPO measurement.
